On June 30, 2026, NPR [retracted](https://www.npr.org/2026/06/30/nx-s1-4622951/samuel-alito-retires) a story reporting that Samuel Alito, age 76, was retiring. There has been similar [speculation](https://www.cbsnews.com/news/supreme-court-justices-alito-thomas-not-retiring-sources-say/) about Clarence Thomas, 78, retiring. Amy Coney Barrett, though only 54, has faced [calls](https://www.dailymail.com/news/article-15942533/trump-amy-coney-barrett-birthright-citizenship.html) from conservative media to resign due to her opinions on recent cases such as birthright citizenship and whether the executive branch of the federal government can overrulse state laws on mail-in balloting.&#x20;

Supreme Court justices serve a [life tenure](https://www.brennancenter.org/our-work/analysis-opinion/life-tenure-us-supreme-court-justices-global-oddity-clear-costs) and are confirmed by a simple majority of the Senate. Thus, if Democrats were to take control of the Senate in the [2026 elections,](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/2026_United_States_Senate_elections) President Donald Trump would need Democratic senators to approve of any of his Supreme Court nominations.&#x20;

At the time of this question, Republicans control the U.S. Senate 53-47, a  majority of 6 seats. However, Metaculus [assigns](https://www.metaculus.com/questions/36370/us-senate-plurality-after-2026-midterms/) Republicans only a 55% chance of retaining the chamber. Republicans face a difficult [map](https://www.270towin.com/2026-senate-election/) in the 2026 cycle, defending contested seats in North Carolina, Georgia, Ohio, Maine, and perhaps Texas and Iowa. Democrats  are defending New Hampshire and Michigan. Historically, the out-of-power party often has an advantage in midterm elections. (See, e.g., the Brookings analysis [What history tells us about the 2026 midterm elections](https://www.brookings.edu/articles/what-history-tells-us-about-the-2026-midterm-elections/).)

North Korea's nuclear program has a long history, but the [first North Korean nuclear test](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/2006_North_Korean_nuclear_test) was conducted On October 9, 2006. Its max yield was smaller than expected, with most sources estimating it as less than 1 kiloton of TNT. Since then, North Korea has conducted five more tests, with [the last one](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/2017_North_Korean_nuclear_test) taking place in 2017 and having a max yield between 50 and 260 kilotons.

At the time of this question, this is a total time period since the first test of 225 months that have passed since the first nuclear test. With 6 tests having been conducted, this is a base rate of 2.7% per month.&#x20;

According to the [2025 Annual Threat Assessment of the US Intelligence Community](https://www.dni.gov/files/ODNI/documents/assessments/ATA-2025-Unclassified-Report.pdf) on page 28 (emphasis added):&#x20;

> Kim remains committed to increasing the number of North Korea’s nuclear warheads and improving its missile capabilities to threaten the Homeland and U.S. forces, citizens, and allies, and to weaken U.S. power in the AsiaPacific region, as evidenced by the pace of the North’s missile flight tests and the regime’s public touting of its uranium enrichment capabilities. **North Korea is probably prepared to conduct a nuclear test** and continues to flight test ICBMs so Kim can threaten the Homeland. Russia is increasingly supporting North Korea’s nuclear status in exchange for Pyongyang’s support to Moscow’s war against Ukraine.

